narrative:
## Modeling RGB Colors

Every pixel on your screen is a mix of red, green, and blue light. Each channel goes from 0 (off) to 255 (full blast).

### The Color Struct

```xiom
type Color = {
  r: Int;
  g: Int;
  b: Int;
}
```

### Creating Colors

```xiom
let crimson = Color{ r: 220; g: 20; b: 60; };
let teal = Color{ r: 0; g: 128; b: 128; };
let snow = Color{ r: 255; g: 255; b: 255; };
```

### Computing Brightness

The average of the three channels gives a rough brightness measure:

```xiom
fn Color.brightness(self) -> Int {
  return (r + g + b) / 3;
}
```

### Checking if a Color is Dark

If the brightness falls below a threshold, the color is dark:

```xiom
fn Color.is_dark(self) -> Bool {
  return self.brightness() < 128;
}
```

### Converting to Hex

Designers use hex strings like `#DC143C` (crimson). Each channel converts to two hex digits:

```xiom
fn to_hex_digit(n: Int) -> Str {
  let digits = "0123456789ABCDEF";
  let hi = n / 16;
  let lo = n % 16;
  return digits + digits;
}

fn Color.to_hex(self) -> Str {
  return "#" + to_hex_digit(r) + to_hex_digit(g) + to_hex_digit(b);
}
```

### Using Color Methods

```xiom
crimson.brightness();  // 100
crimson.is_dark();     // true (100 < 128)
crimson.to_hex();      // "#DC143C"
```

